? What is the meaning of \(2 \frac{7}{10} \mathrm{~cm}\) (the length of the first screw)?

As seen on the ruler, the unit length between two consecutive numbers is divided into 10 equal parts. To get the length \(2 \frac{7}{10} \mathrm{~cm}\), we go from 0 to 2 and then take seven parts of \(\frac{1}{10}\). The length of the screw is 2 cm and \(\frac{7}{10} \mathrm{~cm}\). Similarly, we can make sense of the length \(3 \frac{2}{10} \mathrm{~cm}\).

We read \(2 \frac{7}{10} \mathrm{~cm}\) as two and seven-tenth centimeters, and \(3 \frac{2}{10} \mathrm{~cm}\) as three and two-tenth centimeters.
