\subsection*{3.4 Decimal Place Value}

You may have noticed that whenever we need to measure something more accurately, we split a part into 10 (smaller) equal parts - we split a unit into 10 one-tenths and then split each one-tenth into 10, onehundredths and then we use these smaller parts to measure.

Can we not split a unit into 4 equal parts, 5 equal parts, 8 equal parts, or any other number of equal parts instead?

Yes, we can. The example below compares how the same length is represented when the unit is split into 10 equal parts and when the unit is split into 4 equal parts.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-110.jpg?height=254&width=694&top_left_y=365&top_left_x=1065)

If an even more precise measure is needed, each quarter can further be split into four equal parts. Each part then measures \(\frac{1}{16}\) of a unit, i.e.,
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-110.jpg?height=242&width=690&top_left_y=624&top_left_x=1069)
16 such parts make 1 unit.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-110.jpg?height=237&width=684&top_left_y=960&top_left_x=688)

Then why split a unit into 10 parts every time?
The reason is the special role that 10 plays in the Indian place value system. For a whole number written in the Indian place value system for example, 281 - the place value of 2 is hundreds (100), that of 8 is tens (10), and that of 1 is one (1). Each place value is 10 times bigger than the one immediately to its right. Equivalently, each place value is 10 times smaller than the one immediately to its left:

10 ones make 1 ten,
10 tens make 1 hundred,
10 hundreds make 1 thousand, and so on.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-110.jpg?height=347&width=783&top_left_y=1811&top_left_x=650)

In order to extend this system of writing numbers to quantities smaller than one, we divide one into 10 equal parts. What does this give? It gives one-tenth. Further dividing it into 10 parts gives one-hundredth, and so on.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-111.jpg?height=420&width=1329&top_left_y=308&top_left_x=361)
(?) Can we extend this further?
(?) What will the fraction be when \(\frac{1}{100}\) is split into 10 equal parts?
It will be \(\frac{1}{1000}\), i.e., a thousand such parts make up a unit.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-111.jpg?height=1001&width=1389&top_left_y=1130&top_left_x=323)

Just as when we extend to the left of 10,000 , we get bigger place values at each step, we can also extend to the right of \(\frac{1}{1000}\), getting smaller place values at each step.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-112.jpg?height=377&width=1663&top_left_y=231&top_left_x=221)

This way of writing numbers is called the "decimal system" since it is based on the number 10; "decem" means ten in Latin, which in turn is cognate to the Sanskrit daśha meaning 10, with similar words for 10 occurring across many Indian languages including Odia, Konkani, Marathi, Gujarati, Hindi, Kashmiri, Bodo, and Assamese. We shall learn about other ways of writing numbers in later grades.

\section*{How Big?}

We already know that a hundred 10s make 1000, and a hundred 100s make 10000.

We can ask similar questions about fractional parts:
(a) How many thousandths make one unit?
(b) How many thousandths make one tenth?
(c) How many thousandths make one hundredth?
(d) How many tenths make one ten?
(e) How many hundredths make one ten?

Make a few more questions of this kind and answer them.

\section*{Notation, Writing and Reading of Numbers}

We have been writing numbers in a particular way, say 456 , instead of writing them as \(4 \times 100\) ( 4 hundreds) \(+5 \times 10\) ( 5 tens) \(+6 \times 1\) ( 6 ones). Similarly, can we skip writing tenths and hundredths?
Can the quantity \(4 \frac{2}{10}\) be written as 42 (skipping the \(\frac{1}{10}\) in \(2 \times \frac{1}{10}\) )?

If yes, how would we know if 42 means 4 tens and 2 units or it means 4 units and 2 tenths?

Similarly, 705 could mean:
(a) 7 hundreds, and 0 tens and 5 ones (700 \(+0+5\) )
(b) 7 tens and 0 units and 5 tenths \(\left(70+0+\frac{5}{10}\right)\)
(c) 7 units and 0 tenths and 5 hundredths \(\left(7+\frac{0}{10}+\frac{5}{100}\right)\)

Since these are different quantities, we need to have distinct ways of writing them.

To identify the place value where integers end and the fractional parts start, we use a point or period (‘.’) as a separator, called a decimal point.

The above quantities in decimal notation are then:

\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline Quantity & Decimal Notation \\
\hline 7 hundreds and 5 ones
\[(700+0+5)\] & 705 \\
\hline 7 tens and 5 tenths \(\left(70+0+\frac{5}{10}\right)\) & 70.5 \\
\hline 7 units and 5 hundredths
\[\left(7+0+\frac{5}{100}\right)\] & 7.05 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

These numbers, when shown through place value, are as follows:

\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|l|l|l|}
\hline Decimal number & Hundreds & Tens & Units & Tenths & Hundredths \\
\hline 705 & \(7 \times 100\) & \(0 \times 10\) & \(5 \times 1\) & & \\
\hline 70.5 & & \(7 \times 10\) & \(0 \times 1\) & \(5 \times \frac{1}{10}\) & \\
\hline 7.05 & & & \(7 \times 1\) & \(0 \times \frac{1}{10}\) & \(5 \times \frac{1}{100}\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-113.jpg?height=402&width=1236&top_left_y=1828&top_left_x=412)

Thus decimal notation is a natural extension of the Indian place value system to numbers also having fractional parts. Just as 705 means \(7 \times 100+5 \times 1\), the number 70.5 means \(7 \times 10+5 \times \frac{1}{10}\), and 7.05 means \(7 \times 1+5 \times \frac{1}{100}\).

We have seen how to write numbers using the decimal point (‘.’). But how do we read/say these numbers?

We know that 705 is read as seven hundred and five.
70.5 is read as seventy point five, short for seventy and five-tenths.
7.05 is read as seven point zero five, short for seven and five hundredths.
0.274 is read as zero point two seven four. We don't read it as zero point two hundred and seventy four as 0.274 means 2 one-tenths and 7 one-hundredths and 4 one-thousandths.

Make a place value table similar to the one above. Write each quantity in decimal form and in terms of place value, and read the number:
(a) 2 ones, 3 tenths and 5 hundredths
(b) 1 ten and 5 tenths
(c) 4 ones and 6 hundredths
(d) 1 hundred, 1 one and 1 hundredth
(e) \(\frac{8}{100}\) and \(\frac{9}{10}\)
(f) \(\frac{5}{100}\)
(g) \(\frac{1}{10}\)
(h) \(2 \frac{1}{100}, 4 \frac{1}{10}\) and \(7 \frac{7}{1000}\)

In the chapter on large numbers, we learned how to write 23 hundreds.

23 hundreds \(=23 \times 100=2000+300=2300\).

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline Thousands & Hundreds & Tens & Units \\
\hline & 23 & & \\
\hline 2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

Similarly, 23 tens would be:
23 tens \(=23 \times 10=200+30=230\).

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline Thousands & Hundreds & Tens & Units \\
\hline & & 23 & \\
\hline & 2 & 3 & 0 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

How can we write 234 tenths in decimal form?
\[\begin{aligned}
& 234 \text { tenths }=\frac{234}{10} \\
& =\frac{200}{10}+\frac{30}{10}+\frac{4}{10} \\
& =20+3+\frac{4}{10} \\
& =23.4
\end{aligned}\]

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline Hundreds & Tens & Units & Tenths & Hundredths \\
\hline & & & 234 & \\
\hline & 2 & 3 & 4 & \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
