\subsection*{3.5 Units of Measurement}

\section*{Length Conversion}

We have been using a scale to measure length for a few years. We already know that \(1 \mathrm{~cm}=10 \mathrm{~mm}\) (millimeters).

How many cm is 1 mm ?
\[1 \mathrm{~mm}=\frac{1}{10} \mathrm{~cm}=0.1 \mathrm{~cm} \text { (i.e., one-tenth of a cm). }\]

How many cm is (a) 5 mm ? (b) 12 mm ?
\[\begin{aligned}
5 \mathrm{~mm} & =\frac{5}{10} \mathrm{~cm}=0.5 \mathrm{~cm} \\
12 \mathrm{~mm} & =10 \mathrm{~mm}+2 \mathrm{~mm} \\
& =1 \mathrm{~cm}+\frac{2}{10} \mathrm{~cm} \\
& =1.2 \mathrm{~cm} .
\end{aligned}\]

How many mm is 5.6 cm ? Since each cm has \(10 \mathrm{~mm}, 5.6 \mathrm{~cm}(5 \mathrm{~cm}+\) 0.6 cm ) is 56 mm .

