\section*{Weight Conversion}

Let us look at kilograms (kg). We know that \(1 \mathrm{~kg}=1000\) gram (g). We can say that
\[1 \mathrm{~g}=\frac{1}{1000} \mathrm{~kg}=0.001 \mathrm{~kg} .\]

How many kilograms is 5 g ?
\[5 \mathrm{~g}=\frac{5}{1000} \mathrm{~kg}=0.005 \mathrm{~kg} .\]

How many kilograms is 10 g ?
\[10 \mathrm{~g}=\frac{10}{1000} \mathrm{~kg}=\frac{1}{100} \mathrm{~kg}=0.010 \mathrm{~kg} .\]

As each gram is one-thousandth of a \(\mathrm{kg}, 254 \mathrm{~g}\) can be written as
\[\begin{gathered}
254 \mathrm{~g}=\frac{254}{1000} \mathrm{~kg} \\
=\left(\frac{200}{1000}+\frac{50}{1000}+\frac{4}{1000}\right) \mathrm{kg} \\
=\left(\frac{2}{10}+\frac{5}{100}+\frac{4}{1000}\right) \mathrm{kg} \\
=0.254 \mathrm{~kg} .
\end{gathered}\]

