\subsection*{3.7 Addition and Subtraction of Decimals}

Priya requires 2.7 m of cloth for her skirt, and Shylaja requires 3.5 m for her kurti. What is the total quantity of cloth needed?

We have to find the sum of \(2.7 \mathrm{~m}+3.5 \mathrm{~m}\).
Earlier, we saw how to add \(2 \frac{7}{10}+3 \frac{5}{10}\) (also shown below). Can you carry out the same addition using decimal notation? It is shown below.

Share your observations.
The total quantity of cloth needed is 6.2 m .
\[\begin{array}{r}
2 \frac{7}{10} \\
+3 \frac{5}{10} \\
\hline=5 \frac{12}{10} \\
=6 \frac{2}{10}
\end{array}\]

How much longer is Shylaja's cloth compared to Priya's?
We have to find the difference of \(3.5 \mathrm{~m}-2.7 \mathrm{~m}\). Again, observe how the differences \(3 \frac{5}{10}-2 \frac{7}{10}\) and \(3.5 \mathrm{~m}-2.7 \mathrm{~m}\) are computed.
\[\begin{array}{rrrrr}
3 \frac{5}{10} & \longrightarrow & 2 \frac{15}{10} & 3.5 & \longrightarrow \\
-2 \frac{7}{10} & & -2 \frac{7}{10} & -2.7 & 2.5 \\
& =0 \frac{8}{10} & -2.7 \\
& & & \\
& & & =0.8
\end{array}\]

As you can see, the standard procedure for adding and subtracting whole numbers can be used to add and subtract decimals.

