- the number to the right of ' \(a\) ' will be \(\mathbf{1}\) more than it.
- the number below ' \(a\) ' will be 7 more than it.
- the number diagonal to ' \(a\) ' will be \(\mathbf{8}\) more than it.

So the other numbers in the \(2 \times 2\) square can be represented as shown in the grid. Let us find the diagonal sums; \(a+(a+8)\), and \((a+1)+(a+7)\).

Let us simplify them.

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline\(a\) & \(a+1\) \\
\hline\(a+7\) & \(a+8\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

Since the terms can be added in any order, the brackets can be opened.
\[\begin{aligned}
& a+(a+8)=a+a+8=2 a+8 \\
& (a+1)+(a+7)=a+1+a+7=a+a+1+7=2 a+8
\end{aligned}\]

We see that both diagonal sums are equal to \(2 a+8\) ( 8 more than 2 times \(a\) ).

Verify this expression for diagonal sums by considering any \(2 \times 2\) square and taking its top left number to be ' \(a\) '.

Thus, we have shown that diagonal sums are equal for any value of \(a\), i.e., for any \(2 \times 2\) square!
