What could be an expression describing the rule/formula to find out the number of matchsticks at any step?

The pattern is such that in Step 10, nine \(2 s\) and an added \(3(3+2 \times\) 9) gives the number of matchsticks; in Step 11, ten \(2 s\) and an added \(3(3+2 \times 10)\) gives the number of matchsticks. For step \(y\), what is the expression?

It is: one less than y (i.e. \(y-1\) ) \(2 s\) and \(a 3\).
Therefore, the expression is
\[3+2 \times(y-1) .\]

This expression gives the number of matchsticks in Step \(y\). Now we can find the number of matchsticks at any step quickly.

You might have already noticed that there is a 2 in the first step also, \(3=1+2\). Using this, the expression we get is
\[2 y+1 .\]

Does the above expression also give the number of matchsticks at each step correctly? Are these expressions the same?

We can check by simplifying the expression \(3+2 \times(y-1)\).
\[\begin{gathered}
3+2 \times(y-1)=3+2 y-2 \\
=2 y+1
\end{gathered}\]

