\subsection*{1.2 Congruence of Triangles}

Meera and Rabia have been asked to make a cardboard cutout identical to a triangular frame they have in school. They see that the frame is too big to be traced on a paper and replicated.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-353.jpg?height=423&width=646&top_left_y=1967&top_left_x=711)

What do you think they can do?

\section*{Measuring the Sidelengths}

Can certain measurements of the triangle be used for this? Using a measuring tape, the girls measure the sides of the triangle to be 40 cm , 60 cm , and 80 cm .

Then, Rabia takes out her protractor to measure the angles. She is stopped by Meera.

Meera: The angles of the triangle are not required! With the side lengths we have measured, we can create a triangle congruent to this one.

Do you agree with Meera?

Instead of the lengths being \(40 \mathrm{~cm}, 60 \mathrm{~cm}\), and 80 cm , suppose the sidelengths had been \(4 \mathrm{~cm}, 6 \mathrm{~cm}, 8 \mathrm{~cm}\) (this triangle can fit on our page).

Is this information sufficient to replicate the triangle with the same size and shape? If yes, can you do so?

Rabia: If I were to construct this triangle, I would first draw a line segment having one of the given lengths, say 6 cm , and then draw circles from each of its end points with radii 4 cm and 8 cm . But the circles would intersect at two points, forming two triangles:
\(\triangle \mathrm{ABE}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{ABF}\)
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-354.jpg?height=707&width=780&top_left_y=1558&top_left_x=642)

Rabia: Do these two triangles have the same shape and size? If not, then we will not be sure which of these would actually be congruent to the original triangle we are trying to replicate.

Examine whether \(\triangle \mathrm{ABE}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{ABF}\) are congruent.
For this, you could use one or more of the following methods - tracing and comparing, taking a cutout and superimposing, or observing that AB acts as a line of symmetry due to the 'sameness' of the act of construction above and below this line.

We see that \(\triangle \mathrm{ABE}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{ABF}\) are congruent. From this general construction, we can see that all triangles with the same sidelengths are congruent. Hence, Meera was right when she said that the sidelengths are sufficient to construct a congruent triangle.

Thus, we have the following result:
If two triangles have the same sidelengths, then they are congruent. We call this the SSS (Side Side Side) condition for congruence.

\section*{Conventions to Express Congruence}

The two triangles given below are congruent. How can these two triangles be superimposed? Which vertices of \(\triangle \mathrm{XYZ}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{ABC}\) should we overlap?

This has to be done so that the equal sides overlap. Figure out how.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-355.jpg?height=305&width=328&top_left_y=1290&top_left_x=653)
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-355.jpg?height=255&width=389&top_left_y=1340&top_left_x=1016)

Overlapping Vertex A over Vertex X, Vertex B over Vertex Y and Vertex C over Vertex Z will ensure that equal sides overlap, making the triangles fit exactly over each other.
? Are there other ways of overlapping the vertices so that the triangles fit exactly over each other?

The fact that these triangles are congruent shows that their respective angles are equal:
\[\angle \mathrm{A}=\angle \mathrm{X}, \angle \mathrm{~B}=\angle \mathrm{Y} \text { and } \angle \mathrm{C}=\angle \mathrm{Z}\]

Thus, when two triangles are congruent, there are corresponding vertices, sides and angles which fit exactly over each other when the triangles are made to overlap. In this case, they are
(a) Corresponding Vertices: A and \(\mathrm{X}, \mathrm{B}\) and \(\mathrm{Y}, \mathrm{C}\) and Z
(b) Corresponding Sides: AB and \(\mathrm{XY}, \mathrm{BC}\) and \(\mathrm{YZ}, \mathrm{AC}\) and XZ
(c) Corresponding Angles: \(\angle \mathrm{A}\) and \(\angle \mathrm{X}, \angle \mathrm{B}\) and \(\angle \mathrm{Y}, \angle \mathrm{C}\) and \(\angle \mathrm{Z}\)

To capture this relation that exists when two triangles are congruent, their congruence is written as follows:
\[\triangle \mathrm{ABC} \cong \triangle \mathrm{XYZ}\]
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-356.jpg?height=310&width=454&top_left_y=585&top_left_x=805)

By writing this, we mean that:
- the first vertex in the name of \(\triangle \mathrm{ABC}\) corresponds to the first vertex in the name of \(\triangle \mathrm{XYZ}\),
- the second vertex in the name of \(\triangle \mathrm{ABC}\) corresponds to the second vertex in the name of \(\triangle \mathrm{XYZ}\), and
- similarly with the third vertices in the names of \(\triangle \mathrm{ABC}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{XYZ}\).

By this convention, it is incorrect to write for these two triangles that
\[\triangle \mathrm{ACB} \cong \triangle \mathrm{XYZ} .\]

However, another correct way of saying it is
\[\triangle \mathrm{ACB} \cong \triangle \mathrm{XZY} .\]

Can you identify a pair of congruent triangles below? Why are they congruent?

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-356.jpg?height=368&width=700&top_left_y=1656&top_left_x=683}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig.1.1}
\end{figure}

Consider \(\triangle \mathrm{ABD}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{CDB}\). Since ABCD is a rectangle, we have
\[\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{AB}=\mathrm{CD} \\
& \mathrm{AD}=\mathrm{CB}
\end{aligned}\]

If the remaining sides of \(\triangle \mathrm{ABD}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{CDB}\) have the same length then the SSS condition is satisfied, confirming the congruence of the two triangles. Is this the case?

The remaining side is a common side BD , so the SSS condition holds. Hence, the triangles are congruent.

We know the corresponding sides of the two triangles. We have to identify the corresponding vertices. Can they be the following?
\[\begin{array}{rl}
\triangle \mathrm{ABD} & \triangle \mathrm{CDB} \\
\mathrm{~A} & \mathrm{C} \\
\mathrm{~B} & \mathrm{~B} \\
\mathrm{D} & \mathrm{D}
\end{array}\]

Verify this by superimposing paper cutouts of the triangles obtained from the rectangle ABCD (Fig. 1.1).
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-357.jpg?height=327&width=487&top_left_y=864&top_left_x=787)

We see that this correspondence lays the side \(A B\) of \(\triangle A B D\) over the side CB of \(\Delta \mathrm{CDB}\). But these sides need not be equal, and hence, this superimposition will not establish congruence.

Identify the correct correspondence of vertices and express the congruence between the two triangles.

