\section*{Measuring Two Angles and a Non-Included Side}

The following triangles \(\triangle \mathrm{ABC}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{XYZ}\) are such that \(\angle \mathrm{A}=\angle \mathrm{X}=35^{\circ}\), \(\angle \mathrm{C}=\angle \mathrm{Z}=75^{\circ}\), and \(\mathrm{BC}=\mathrm{YZ}=4 \mathrm{~cm}\). Are the triangles congruent? Give a reason.

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-364.jpg?height=593&width=1091&top_left_y=232&top_left_x=485}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Fig.1.2}
\end{figure}

How do we proceed with this problem? Here is a method.
What are the measures of \(\angle \mathrm{B}\) and \(\angle \mathrm{Y}\) ?
We know that the sum of the angles of a triangle is \(180^{\circ}\).
So \(\angle \mathrm{B}+35^{\circ}+75^{\circ}=180^{\circ}\),
or \(\angle \mathrm{B}+110^{\circ}=180^{\circ}\)
Thus, \(\angle \mathrm{B}=70^{\circ}\).
Similarly, \(\angle \mathrm{Y}\) is also \(70^{\circ}\).
Thus, we have \(\angle \mathrm{B}=\angle \mathrm{Y}\).
(?) Does this help in showing that \(\triangle \mathrm{ABC}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{XYZ}\) are congruent?
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-364.jpg?height=578&width=397&top_left_y=1520&top_left_x=487)
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-364.jpg?height=591&width=397&top_left_y=1512&top_left_x=1179)

These two triangles now satisfy the ASA condition with
\[\begin{aligned}
& \angle \mathrm{B}=\angle \mathrm{Y} \\
& \mathrm{BC}=\mathrm{YZ} \\
& \angle \mathrm{C}=\angle \mathrm{Z}
\end{aligned}\]

So, \(\triangle \mathrm{ABC} \cong \triangle \mathrm{XYZ}\).

In Fig. 1.2, the equalities are between two angles and the non-included side of the two triangles. This condition is referred to as the AAS (Angle Angle Side) condition.

As we have seen, the AAS condition guarantees congruence.
We have seen that the SSA condition doesn't always guarantee congruence. However, there are some special cases when SSA does guarantee congruence. Here is one such important case.

\section*{Measuring Two Sides in a Right Triangle}
\(\triangle \mathrm{ABC}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{XYZ}\) are right-angled triangles such that \(\mathrm{BC}=\mathrm{YZ}=4 \mathrm{~cm}\), \(\angle \mathrm{B}=\angle \mathrm{Y}=90^{\circ}\) and \(\mathrm{AC}=\mathrm{XZ}=5 \mathrm{~cm}\). Are they congruent?

Can there exist non-congruent triangles having these measurements? Construct and find out.

Looking at the rough diagram helps in planning the construction.

Step 1: Draw the base QR of length 4 cm .
Step 2: Draw a line \(l\) perpendicular to QR from Q .
Step 3: From R, cut an arc on line \(l\) of radius 5 cm .
Step 4: Let P be the point at which the arc intersects the line \(l\). Join PR.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-365.jpg?height=471&width=521&top_left_y=1840&top_left_x=770)
\(\triangle \mathrm{PQR}\) is the required triangle.

Consider the downward extension of line \(l\) below QR. Would the arc from R meet this line downwards as well (as in the case of triangle construction when the sidelengths are given)? If so, would this lead to a triangle whose size and shape are different from \(\triangle \mathrm{PQR}\), and yet has the given measurements?

It can be seen that the other triangle we get below is also congruent to \(\triangle \mathrm{PQR}\). Why? Therefore, all triangles having these measurements will be congruent to each other.

Thus, we conclude that \(\triangle \mathrm{ABC} \cong \triangle \mathrm{XYZ}\).
In the case that we have considered, the parts that are equal to their corresponding parts in another triangle are
(a) the right angle
(b) two other sides, one of which is opposite to the right angle. This side is called the hypotenuse.
This is called the RHS (Right Hypotenuse Side) condition, and is one more condition for congruence.

\section*{Conditions that are sufficient to guarantee congruence}

From the discussions so far, we can see that two triangles are congruent if any of the following conditions are satisfied:
(a) SSS condition
(b) SAS condition
(c) ASA condition
(d) AAS condition
(e) RHS condition

\subsection*{1.3 Angles of Isosceles and Equilateral Triangles}

Congruence is a very powerful tool for studying properties of geometric figures. Let us use it to discover an important property of isosceles triangles.
\(\triangle \mathrm{ABC}\) is isosceles with \(\mathrm{AB}=\mathrm{AC}\), and \(\angle \mathrm{A}=80\). What can we say about \(\angle \mathrm{B}\) and \(\angle \mathrm{C}\) ?

Construct the altitude from A to BC.

We have,
\[\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{AB} & =\mathrm{AC} \text { (given) } \\
\angle \mathrm{ADB}=\angle \mathrm{ADC} & =90^{\circ} \text { (from construction) }
\end{aligned}\]

AD is a common side of the two triangles \(\triangle \mathrm{ADB}\) and \(\triangle \mathrm{ADC}\).
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-367.jpg?height=372&width=559&top_left_y=470&top_left_x=751)

Thus, the triangles satisfy the RHS condition. Hence, \(\triangle \mathrm{ADB} \cong \triangle \mathrm{ADC}\).
This shows that \(\angle \mathrm{B}=\angle \mathrm{C}\), as they are corresponding parts of congruent triangles.

Thus, the angles opposite to equal sides are equal.
Can you use this fact to find \(\angle \mathrm{B}\) and \(\angle \mathrm{C}\) ?

\section*{Angles in an Equilateral Triangle}

Equilateral triangles are those in which all the sides have equal lengths.
What can we say about their angles?
We can use the recently discovered fact that angles opposite to equal sides are equal.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-367.jpg?height=382&width=451&top_left_y=1552&top_left_x=802)

The sides AB and AC are equal. So \(\angle \mathrm{B}=\angle \mathrm{C}\).
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-367.jpg?height=384&width=446&top_left_y=2040&top_left_x=809)

Similarly, the sides AB and BC are equal. So \(\angle \mathrm{A}=\angle \mathrm{C}\).
So, all the three angles of an equilateral triangle are equal, just like their sides.

What could be their measures?
As the three angles should add up to \(180^{\circ}\), we have \(3 \times\) angle in an equilateral triangle \(=180^{\circ}\).
So each angle is \(60^{\circ}\).

Verify this by construction.
Thus, just using the notion of congruence, we have deduced that the angles of an equilateral triangle are all \(60^{\circ}\).

