\subsection*{4.4 Look Before You Leap!}

Did you know that it takes the Earth 365.2422 days to go around the Sun and not 365 days? For our convenience, we consider 365 days as a year in a calendar. We are talking about Gregorian calendar.

This means that, after one calendar year or 365 days, the Earth still needs 0.2422 more days to complete one full revolution around the Sun. This doesn't seem like much. But what happens
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-437.jpg?height=590&width=761&top_left_y=340&top_left_x=1133)
after 100 such calendar years?

Using our understanding of decimal multiplication,
\[0.2422 \times 100=24.22 \text { days } .\]

After 100 calendar years, the Earth will need 24.22 more days to complete its 100th revolution around the Sun.

In your Science classes, you have learnt that we experience seasons because of the Earth's tilt in axis and its revolution around the Sun. If our calendar does not accurately indicate the position of the Earth around the Sun, our seasons and our annual calendar will not match!

To correct this problem, the idea of a leap year was introduced. Every fourth year, one additional day is added to the calendar year.

\section*{Making an Adjustment}
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-437.jpg?height=694&width=1313&top_left_y=1619&top_left_x=368)

Is the year divisible by 4 ?

The year has 366 days

The year has 365 days

Do you know which month has this extra day?
Let us see how this solution works.

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline Year 1 & Year 2 & Year 3 & Year 4 & Year 5 & Year 6 & Year 7 & Year 8 & \(\ldots\) \\
\hline 365 & 365 & 365 & 366 & 365 & 365 & 365 & 366 & \(\ldots\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

Looking at the above sequence, the number of days after 4 calendar years is
\[4 \times 365+1=1461 \text { days. }\]

What is the number of days that the Earth needs to make 4 full revolutions around the Sun?
\[4 \times 365.2422=1460.9688 \text { days } .\]

With this new scheme of adding one extra day every 4th year, what is the number of days in 100 calendar years? Can you write an expression to calculate that number?
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-438.jpg?height=178&width=182&top_left_y=1386&top_left_x=1686)

Here is one way to form the expression. Each calendar year has 365 days. In 100 calendar years, the number of days is \(100 \times 365\). But years that are divisible by 4 have one extra day.

How many years are divisible by 4 in 100 years?
So, the number of days in 100 calendar years is,
\[\left(100 \times 365+\frac{100}{4} \times 1\right)=36,525 \text { days } .\]

Can you form different expressions for the same question?
The actual number of days that the Earth takes to go around the Sun 100 times is,
\[100 \times 365.2422=36,524.22 \text { days } .\]

Thus, by adding a day every fourth year, after 100 years, the calendar days are more than the actual number of days taken by the Earth to go around the Sun. We have overcompensated.

So, the good people who designed calendars decided that they will not add 1 extra day in every hundredth year!

