A statement of equality between two algebraic expressions is called an equation. Nowadays, when using symbols, we write an equation as two algebraic expressions with an equal sign '=' between them.

Here are some more examples of equations:
\[\begin{gathered}
3 x+4=7 \\
20=y-3 \\
\frac{a}{3}=50 \\
2 z+4=5 z-14 \text { etc. }
\end{gathered}\]

The process of finding the value(s) of the letter-numbers for which the equality holds, or for which the value of the Left Hand Side (LHS) of the equation becomes equal to the Right Hand Side (RHS) of the equation, is called solving the equation.

As we saw in the matchstick problem, framing an equation using an unknown quantity as a letter-number can help us find its value.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/cfd9cba9-077b-4f37-a732-40ee0519eab8-516.jpg?height=292&width=838&top_left_y=2089&top_left_x=979)

