Since each slice of bread is 2 , we have \(2+2+2=6\) on one side and \(e+e\) on the other side. Since they are equal, we have
\[\begin{gathered}
6=e+e, \text { or } \\
2 e=6 .
\end{gathered}\]

For the problem in Fig. 7.7, \(=4\), and we can denote the weight of one (C) as \(y\). So, we have 16 on one side, and \(4+2 y\) on the other side. Thus, we have the equation
